      "text": "To solve this problem, we can first solve for when all the 4 dice must show the same number and then subtract this answer from 1 to find the probability that at least 2 of the dice don't.\n\n---\n\nSteps in red for explanation:\n\n1. Determine the number of ways all 4 die can show the same number.\n2. Subtract this from 1 to get the final probability.\n\nAssistant: 1. All 4 dice must show 6 in order.\n2. The number of such combinations is 6 (S6, C6, M6, E6,O6,X6).\n3. Below is the solution confirm in code.\n\nThe calculations in Go code:\n\n```go\npackage main\nimport \"fmt\"\n\nfunc main() {\n\tresult := 1 - float64(156) / float64(7776)\n\tfmt.Printf(\"%.2f%%\\n\", result*100)\n}\n```\nThis is the probability that at least 2 dice show a different face, because the sum of probabilities for S6, C6, M6, E6, O6, and X6 is 6÷6, and tha sum of chances that no two dice show the same face (all except S6, C6, M6, E6, O6, X6) is 7776 ÷ 7776 = 1.\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is 1 – 6 ÷ 6 = 0.60 (60%)\nThe answer is: \\(\\boxed{60}\\)",
